Lab 3: Containerized Deep Learning Deployment
Objective
Package a deep learning model inside a Docker container and deploy it locally on Kubernetes to simulate scalable AI application deployment — all without using cloud resources.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure the following tools are installed:

Docker: sudo apt install docker.io -y

Kubernetes (via Minikube or MicroK8s)

For Minikube:

sudo apt install conntrack -y
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube
minikube start --driver=docker
For MicroK8s:

sudo snap install microk8s --classic
sudo microk8s status --wait-ready
Steps
Step 1: Create a Simple Deep Learning API
Create a project folder:

mkdir -p ~/dl-k8s-lab/model-api
cd ~/dl-k8s-lab/model-api
Create a basic Flask API (app.py):

from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
import joblib

app = Flask(__name__)
model = joblib.load("iris_model.pkl")

@app.route("/predict", methods=["POST"])
def predict():
    data = request.get_json()
    prediction = model.predict([data["features"]])
    return jsonify({"prediction": int(prediction[0])})

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)
Copy your trained model (iris_model.pkl) from Lab 2 into this folder.

Create a requirements.txt:

flask
scikit-learn
joblib
Step 2: Create a Dockerfile
Create a Dockerfile:

FROM python:3.9-slim

WORKDIR /app
COPY . /app

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
Build the Docker image:

docker build -t iris-model-api .
Run the container:

docker run -p 5000:5000 iris-model-api
Test it locally:

curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/predict \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"features": [5.1, 3.5, 1.4, 0.2]}'
Step 3: Deploy the Container on Kubernetes (Minikube or MicroK8s)
Create a deployment file deployment.yaml:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: iris-model
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: iris-model
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: iris-model
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: iris-model
        image: iris-model-api
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5000
Create a service file service.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: iris-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: iris-model
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 5000
  type: NodePort
Apply the Kubernetes configuration:

For Minikube:

minikube start
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
minikube service iris-service
For MicroK8s:

microk8s kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
microk8s kubectl apply -f service.yaml
microk8s kubectl get services
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Packaged a deep learning model inside a Docker container

Exposed the model via a Flask API

Deployed the container on a local Kubernetes cluster (Minikube or MicroK8s)

This local simulation mirrors real-world cloud-based model deployments and prepares you to scale ML applications reliably in production environments — without needing cloud access.
